/*!
\page qtquick3dphysics-queries.html
\title Qt Quick 3D Physics Scene Queries
\brief Overview of scene query mechanics, types, and best practices in Qt
Quick 3D Physics.

Scene queries allow applications to test spatial relationships and detect
collisions programmatically within a \l PhysicsWorld. They enable mechanics
such as line-of-sight checks, spatial probing, volume clearance testing, and
interactive picking without relying on standard physical contact callbacks.

\section1 Types of Scene Queries

Qt Quick 3D Physics provides three main query modes, each optimized for different
spatial operations:

\table
\header
\li Query Type
\li Operation
\li Typical Use Cases
\row
\li \b Raycast
\li Casts an infinitely thin line segment along a direction vector.
\li Line-of-sight checks, weapon ballistics, laser pointers, user object
  picking.
\row
\li \b Sweep
\li Sweeps a 3D volume (\l CollisionShape) from its scene position along a linear
  direction vector.
\li Character volume clearance checks, wall-sliding, predictive movement
  checks.
\row
\li \b Overlap
\li Tests a stationary 3D volume (\l CollisionShape) at its current scene position.
\li Spawn-point occupation, area-of-effect (AoE) damage, proximity
  detection.
\endtable

Each query mode is split into three performance variants depending on the detail
level required:

\list
\li \b {Test Queries} (\c testRaycastQuery, \c testSweepQuery,
  \c testOverlapQuery):
  Boolean checks that terminate immediately upon finding the first
  intersection. They return no detailed hit data, making them the fastest
  option for occlusion or clearance validation.
\li \b {Single Queries} (\c singleRaycastQuery, \c singleSweepQuery):
  Return detailed information (\l locationHit, \l queryHit)
  for the \b closest intersecting body.
\li \b {Multi Queries} (\c multiRaycastQuery, \c multiSweepQuery,
  \c multiOverlapQuery):
  Return a list of hit objects for all intersecting bodies along the query
  path or volume.
\endlist

\section1 Query Mechanics and Key Nuances

When using scene queries, several system behaviors should be taken
into consideration.

\section2 Bodies Excluded from Queries

Trigger bodies, such as \l TriggerBody, are not physical colliders and never appear in
scene query results. Use an overlap query if you need the equivalent of a trigger volume
that you can poll on demand.

\section2 Filter Groups Do Not Apply

The \l {PhysicsNode::filterGroup} {filterGroup} and
\l {PhysicsNode::filterIgnoreGroups} {filterIgnoreGroups} properties control which bodies
collide with each other during simulation. They have no effect on scene queries: a query
evaluates every collider matching its \c includeStatic and \c includeDynamic arguments,
regardless of the filter groups involved. If a query needs to ignore specific bodies,
filter the results after the query returns.

\section2 Query Shape Transforms

For \b Sweep and \b Overlap queries, the provided \l CollisionShape defines not only
the geometry and scale, but also the starting position and orientation for the query.
The shape's scene transform (including parent node transformations) is baked directly
into the query execution.

\section2 Asynchronous Frame Timing and Latency

The physics simulation steps concurrently alongside QML scene graph rendering.
Scene queries executed while a physics step is in flight reflect object
positions, poses, and bounding structures from the \e {last completed physics
frame}.

Consequently, queries triggered inside QML event handlers (such as \c onClicked
or a \c Timer) operate on the state of the last completed step. That is the same
state the scene's visual representation reflects, so a query result agrees with
what is currently rendered; it does not account for motion occurring during the
step being computed.

\section2 Triangle Mesh Multi-Hit Behavior

Multi-hit queries against complex triangle meshes or heightfield shapes yield a
single intersection result per mesh object (the closest entry point). Individual
triangles of a single mesh are not reported as separate hits.

Furthermore, rays or volume sweeps that originate from \e inside a mesh or exit
through back-facing surface triangles will not report hits for that mesh.

\section2 Result Ordering in Multi-Queries

Hit objects returned by multi-queries (\c multiRaycastQuery, \c multiSweepQuery,
\c multiOverlapQuery) are populated based on spatial tree traversal order.
\b {The results in the returned list are not guaranteed to be ordered by
distance.} If a distance-sorted list is required, applications should sort the
resulting list explicitly.

\section2 Minimum Translation Distance (MTD) in Initial Overlaps

By default, sweep queries handle initial overlaps with existing colliders using
Minimum Translation Distance (MTD). If a query shape already overlaps a body at its
starting position, the hit result reports a negative \c distance (representing the
penetration depth), along with the separation \c position and \c normal.

\section2 Character Controller Query Hits

When scene queries hit a \l CharacterController, the returned hit structure
(\l queryHit, or \l locationHit) will contain a valid \c body, but
its \c shape property will be \c null. This occurs because the underlying
character proxy shape does not maintain individual shape metadata. Applications
should check if \c shape is \c null before accessing shape-specific properties.

\section1 Filtering and Optimization Best Practices

To achieve optimal performance when using scene queries:

\list
\li \b {Use Test Queries First:} Use \c testRaycastQuery or \c testSweepQuery
  when you only need a \c true / \c false answer (e.g., AI visibility
  checks). They bypass allocation and computation of hit geometry.
\li \b {Filter Body Types:} Constrain query searches using the \c includeStatic
  and \c includeDynamic parameters. If a raycast only needs to hit dynamic
  objects, setting \c includeStatic to \c false reduces traversal overhead.
\li \b {Limit Distance:} Keep \c maxDistance as short as practical to minimize
  the number of candidate bounds evaluated by the spatial acceleration
  structures.
\endlist

\section1 Example
The \l {Qt Quick 3D Physics - Queries Example} shows how to get physical bodies from the scene.

\sa PhysicsWorld, locationHit, queryHit
*/
